{
 "id": "targeting-intersect",
 "title": "Ad Targeting: Intersect Posting Lists",
 "pattern": "two-pointers",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>An ad server picks campaigns for a request by its targeting attributes. It keeps an <b>inverted index</b>: for each attribute, such as <code>\"country=SG\"</code> or <code>\"device=ios\"</code>, a sorted list of the campaign ids that target it.</p><p>You get <code>postings</code>, a dict from attribute string to a list of <b>distinct</b> campaign ids in <b>ascending</b> order, and <code>required</code>, a list of attribute strings. A campaign matches when its id appears in the list of <b>every</b> required attribute.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(postings, required)</code> that returns the sorted list of matching campaign ids.</p><ul><li>If any required attribute is not a key of <code>postings</code>, no campaign can match: return <code>[]</code>.</li><li>If <code>required</code> is empty, return <code>[]</code> (the server never serves untargeted requests from this index).</li><li><code>required</code> may name the same attribute more than once.</li><li>Do not modify the input lists.</li></ul>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "postings = {\"country=SG\": [1, 3, 5, 7, 9], \"device=ios\": [3, 4, 5, 9], \"age=25-34\": [2, 3, 9, 10]}, required = [\"country=SG\", \"device=ios\", \"age=25-34\"]",
   "output": "[3, 9]",
   "why": "3 and 9 are in all three lists. 5 is missing from age=25-34."
  },
  {
   "input": "postings = {\"country=SG\": [1, 2, 3], \"device=ios\": [2, 3]}, required = [\"country=SG\", \"lang=th\"]",
   "output": "[]",
   "why": "No campaign targets lang=th, so nothing can match."
  },
  {
   "input": "postings = {\"country=SG\": [4, 8, 15], \"device=web\": [8]}, required = [\"country=SG\"]",
   "output": "[4, 8, 15]",
   "why": "One attribute: its whole list matches."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "0 &le; len(postings) &le; 100",
  "0 &le; len(required) &le; 20",
  "0 &le; length of each list &le; 30,000; ids are distinct and ascending",
  "1 &le; campaign id &le; 10^9"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(postings, required):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What is the answer, and what are the special cases?",
   "a": "The ids common to all the required lists, in ascending order. A missing attribute means an empty list, so the answer is <code>[]</code>. An empty <code>required</code> is defined as <code>[]</code>. Repeating an attribute changes nothing, because <code>A &amp; A = A</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Intersect <code>[1, 3, 5, 7, 9]</code> and <code>[3, 4, 5, 9]</code> with two fingers.",
   "a": "1 &lt; 3: move left. 3 = 3: keep 3, move both. 5 &gt; 4: move right. 5 = 5: keep. 7 &lt; 9: move left. 9 = 9: keep. Result <code>[3, 5, 9]</code>. Then <code>[3, 5, 9]</code> with <code>[2, 3, 9, 10]</code> gives <code>[3, 9]</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the first idea that works?",
   "a": "Turn every list into a set, intersect, and sort the result. Correct and linear, but it hashes every id in every list and throws away the fact that the lists are already sorted. A truly naive version tests each id with <code>in</code> on the other lists, which is O(n) per test."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Which pattern?",
   "a": "<b>Two pointers</b> on sorted lists, the merge step of merge sort. Sort the lists by length and start from the shortest: the running result can never be longer than the shortest list, so every later merge is cheaper, and you can stop as soon as it is empty."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Empty <code>required</code>: return <code>[]</code>.</li><li>Look up each attribute; if one is missing, return <code>[]</code>.</li><li>Sort the lists by length.</li><li><code>result = copy of the shortest</code>; for each other list, merge with two pointers, keeping equal ids.</li><li>Stop early if <code>result</code> is empty. Return it.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "A missing attribute (a <code>KeyError</code> if you index blindly). An attribute present with an empty list. The same attribute twice. No overlap at all. A single attribute (return a copy, not the original list, so the caller cannot mutate the index). Empty <code>required</code>."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "The lists are already sorted. How does merge sort combine two sorted lists? Keep only the ids that appear in both.",
  "Intersect two at a time: <code>result = merge(result, next_list)</code>. The order you pick the lists in changes the cost, not the answer.",
  "Sort the lists by <code>len</code> and start from the shortest. Check for missing attributes first, and stop as soon as <code>result</code> is empty."
 ],
 "solution": "def intersect_two(a, b):\n    # Classic merge step on two sorted lists of distinct ids.\n    i = 0\n    j = 0\n    common = []\n    while i < len(a) and j < len(b):\n        if a[i] == b[j]:\n            common.append(a[i])\n            i += 1\n            j += 1\n        elif a[i] < b[j]:\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            j += 1\n    return common\n\ndef solution(postings, required):\n    if not required:\n        return []\n    lists = []\n    for attr in required:\n        if attr not in postings:\n            return []                  # nobody matches an unknown attribute\n        lists.append(postings[attr])\n    lists.sort(key=len)                # shortest first keeps every partial result small\n    result = list(lists[0])\n    for other in lists[1:]:\n        if not result:\n            break                      # nothing left to match\n        result = intersect_two(result, other)\n    return result\n",
 "brute": "def solution(postings, required):\n    # Set intersection: simple and also linear, but it hashes every id\n    # in every list and needs a final sort.\n    if not required:\n        return []\n    common = None\n    for attr in required:\n        ids = set(postings.get(attr, []))\n        common = ids if common is None else common & ids\n    return sorted(common)\n",
 "explain": "<p>Example 1. Lengths: country=SG 5, device=ios 4, age=25-34 4. Sorted by length: device=ios, age=25-34, country=SG.</p><ul><li>Start with <code>[3, 4, 5, 9]</code>.</li><li>Merge with <code>[2, 3, 9, 10]</code>: 2 is skipped, 3 kept, 4 and 5 skipped, 9 kept: <code>[3, 9]</code>.</li><li>Merge with <code>[1, 3, 5, 7, 9]</code>: 3 kept, 9 kept: <code>[3, 9]</code>.</li></ul><p>Each merge costs O(len(result) + len(other)), so the total is O(sum of lengths). Starting from the shortest list keeps <code>len(result)</code> small and lets the loop stop early: in the merge, once the short side runs out, you stop, even if the long list has thousands of ids left.</p><p>When one list is much shorter (m ids) than the other (n ids), <b>galloping</b> is faster: for each id in the short list, binary-search the long one with <code>bisect.bisect_left</code>, starting from the last position. That costs O(m log n) instead of O(m + n). Search engines and ad indexes use this, often with skip pointers stored in the posting lists.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(L log L + total length) time for L required lists (O(m log n) per pair with galloping); O(shortest list) extra space",
