  "kind": "fundamentals",
  "from": "LC 349 Intersection of Two Arrays, extended to k sorted posting lists",
  "talk": [
   "O(sum of lengths): each merge step advances at least one pointer, and I sort the lists by <code>len</code> first so the running result is never longer than the shortest list.",
   "Shortest first matters: intersecting two 30,000-id lists first can leave a big middle result, while starting from a 200-id list caps every later merge's left side at 200 and often stops early.",
   "Galloping: when the short list has m ids and the long one n, binary search with <code>bisect_left(other, x, lo)</code> costs O(m log n), which beats O(m + n) once n is much bigger than m.",
   "Edge cases: missing attribute (<code>in postings</code> check, no <code>KeyError</code>), an empty list, repeated attributes, empty <code>required</code>, and returning a copy so the index is never mutated."
  ],
  "extensions": [
   "Implement the galloping version with <code>bisect</code>. When does it lose to the plain two-pointer merge?",
   "Support OR inside a group: <code>[[\"country=SG\", \"country=MY\"], [\"device=ios\"]]</code> means (SG or MY) and ios. Merge-union each group, then intersect the groups.",
   "Add exclusions such as <code>not device=web</code>: a sorted difference with two pointers, done after the intersections so the left side is small.",
   "The lists hold millions of ids each: store them as compressed blocks with skip pointers, or as bitmaps (roaring-style), and intersect with bitwise AND.",
   "Only the top 10 campaigns by bid are needed. Can you stop before the full intersection is built?"
